This article examines spatially nonlocal diffusion models where interactions are governed by a function $\omega$ in the following integral equation:
  \begin{align*}
  \Omega\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\frac{u(x)-u(y)}{x-y}\omega(x-y)\mathrm{d}y\right)=f.
  \end{align*}
  The article specifically investigates the properties of these models in a bounded domain, and in the case where the function $\omega$ is confined within a bounded range, the model demonstrates classical diffusion characteristics similar to those found in the Poisson equation. Conversely, if the function $\omega$ decays rapidly, the model displays fractal-like behavior.

